A program may name `sngl:async` and render a timer, on a target whose own
package needs the same two ids.

These collided by construction. fyne's timer hands its tick to a host scheduler
and has to be back on the drawing thread before it touches a widget, so the
platform package declared `async.post` and `async.spawn` for itself; `sngl:async`
declares them for everyone. `ir.RegisterIntrinsic` panics on a duplicate, so the
two together took the compiler down at load:

	panic: ir: intrinsic "async.spawn" is declared twice,
	as sngl:platform/fyne.spawn and as sngl:async.spawn

which is the whole reason the package and the timer are one merge request rather
than two. fyne names `sngl:async` now instead of declaring its own, so there is
one declaration of each id and the platform answers it for both callers: the
program's `async.post` below and the tick inside the ticker's goroutine both
come out as `fyne.Do`.

The assertion is that this program builds at all -- reverted, the harness does
not fail an assertion, it panics before reaching one.

deny out/go/fyne/model.go `(?m)^\s*post\(` -- the hand-over is the platform's own fyne.Do; a call to a function named `post` means the id stopped resolving and nothing answered it

-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"
import time "sngl:time"
import async "sngl:async"

output {
    go {
        fyne
    }
}

ui.window(title="t") {
    var n = 0

    time.timer(interval=1000ms, @tick { n = n + 1 })
    ui.button(text="go", @click {
        async.post(func() {
            n = n + 1
        })
    })
    ui.text(value=string(n))
}
-- out/go/fyne/model.go --
